In article <9325@microsoft.UUCP> t-jlee@microsoft.UUCP (Johnny Lee) writes:
>In article <984@madnix.UUCP> jeffm@madnix.UUCP (Jeff Metzner) writes:
>>(3)  Invisiblity doesn't work right.  The cursor disappears, and the position
>>     of the invisible character can't be seen.
>
>It was designed that way. To me invisible means invisible (but with
>the changes I plan to make for pl7, that won't be exactly true anymore).
>If enough people complain, this "feature" can change.

I'd claim that you ought to be able to see where you are if you are
invisible.  Consider what the case if you were to become invisible in
'reality'.  You'd still be able to see your surroundings, and so to
tell where you were, no?

On the other hand, you probably *shouldn't* be able to tell what your
character's position is if you are blind.

